WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.020
A mouse has no opsin, yet light changes its firing and turning.

2
00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:06.360
What did the light change?

3
00:00:09.833 --> 00:00:15.113
In wild-type mice, continuous striatal light suppressed firing and biased turning.

4
00:00:16.473 --> 00:00:20.273
Illumination had changed tissue without a light-sensitive construct.

5
00:00:21.633 --> 00:00:26.433
A light-on comparison therefore changes at least two things: the intended neural

6
00:00:26.433 --> 00:00:28.193
activity and the light itself.

7
00:00:29.333 --> 00:00:33.333
A recording can show that activity and behaviour vary together.

8
00:00:34.713 --> 00:00:36.933
A manipulation asks a different question.

9
00:00:38.673 --> 00:00:42.733
Driving chosen cells tests whether their activity is sufficient for the measured

10
00:00:42.733 --> 00:00:47.053
change. Suppressing them tests whether it is necessary.

11
00:00:48.433 --> 00:00:51.133
Each answer belongs only to the tested conditions.

12
00:00:52.283 --> 00:00:55.703
To separate the two causes, cross construct with light.

13
00:00:56.923 --> 00:00:59.983
Put opsin and fluorophore-only animals in two rows.

14
00:01:01.243 --> 00:01:03.243
Put light off and light on in two columns.

15
00:01:04.603 --> 00:01:08.303
Now every effect of illumination has a matched place to appear.

16
00:01:09.383 --> 00:01:13.243
You have compared light on with light off only in opsin animals.

17
00:01:14.363 --> 00:01:18.623
Which missing comparison would reveal whether light itself moved the behaviour?

18
00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:27.700
Give fluorophore-only animals the same light.

19
00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:31.020
First measure the light effect in each row.

20
00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:33.700
Then compare those two effects.

21
00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:36.900
The shared light effect cancels.

22
00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:42.700
The remainder is the extra light-linked effect associated with carrying the opsin.

23
00:01:43.733 --> 00:01:47.413
Read the board as two differences, not four separate boxes.

24
00:01:48.913 --> 00:01:50.973
Measure the light effect in opsin animals.

25
00:01:52.413 --> 00:01:54.453
Measure it again in fluorophore animals.

26
00:01:55.833 --> 00:01:57.713
Subtract the second effect from the first.

27
00:01:58.933 --> 00:02:03.073
This difference of differences identifies the opsin-linked remainder.

28
00:02:05.183 --> 00:02:09.823
That remainder is not a license to say the circuit controls the behaviour.

29
00:02:11.303 --> 00:02:14.563
Name the cells, the projection, the time window and the assay.

30
00:02:16.223 --> 00:02:17.623
Activation tests sufficiency.

31
00:02:19.143 --> 00:02:20.463
Silencing tests necessity.

32
00:02:21.943 --> 00:02:23.883
Neither says these cells are the only route.

33
00:02:24.933 --> 00:02:27.693
A light spot does not name a pathway by itself.

34
00:02:29.433 --> 00:02:34.273
Terminal illumination can also excite labelled axons that only pass through the

35
00:02:34.273 --> 00:02:38.693
field. It can drive activity back toward the parent cell bodies.

36
00:02:40.073 --> 00:02:45.533
A local receptor block tests whether transmitter action there is required for the

37
00:02:45.533 --> 00:02:46.893
measured behavioural effect.

38
00:02:48.017 --> 00:02:51.517
Finally, verify what the manipulation actually did.

39
00:02:53.037 --> 00:02:54.297
Record during and after the light.

40
00:02:55.617 --> 00:02:57.637
Confirm expression and fibre placement.

41
00:02:59.097 --> 00:03:01.577
Randomize allocation and blind the scoring.

42
00:03:02.877 --> 00:03:07.917
Only when the manipulation and its controls work can the matched contrast identify

43
00:03:07.917 --> 00:03:12.537
the effect. A claim can be no broader than that contrast.
